{"episodes":{"211892":"A broad upper level low centered offshore from northern California produced numerous thunderstorms in southern Oregon. One long-lived thunderstorm produced damaging winds across a portion of Jackson and Josephine counties.","211992":"During the late evening hours of May 3rd, warm air advection and low-level jet convergence led to scattered thunderstorm development south of the I-72 corridor. Most of the storms remained below severe limits, but some produced dime to quarter-sized hail.","212797":"Scattered thunderstorms moved across parts of southern Lake Michigan during the late afternoon and evening of May 4th producing isolated severe wind gusts.","213118":"A cold front was approaching the region during the evening of May 3rd. Concurrently, a low-level jet was ramping up in the area. This resulted in the development of thunderstorms, some of which became severe. One of these thunderstorms produced large hail in Warren and Lincoln Counties.","213119":"A cold front was approaching the region during the evening of May 3rd. Concurrently, a low-level jet was ramping up in the area. This resulted in the development of thunderstorms, some of which became severe and produced large hail in Macoupin and Madison counties.","213120":"During the afternoon of May 4th, a cold front was approaching the region from the northwest. As the low-level jet ramped up in the late afternoon, convection blossomed. One thunderstorm was able to become severe and produce large hail in Madison and Bond counties.","213143":"Severe thunderstorms on the evening of May 4 produced wind gusts in excess of 50 mph and hail.","213145":"Isolated severe thunderstorms produced small hail and gusty wind.","213396":"A cluster of thunderstorms moving east to slightly northeast ahead of a strong cold front approaching from the north produced a 60 mph wind gust southeast of Quinter.","213501":"Scattered thunderstorms developed along a front that was moving across the area during the morning afternoon hours on May 4th.  These storms produced gusts of 40 to 50 MPH but a few of these storms became severe and produced hail to 1 inch in diameter and damaging straight-line winds.","213503":"Scattered thunderstorms developed along a front that was moving across the area during the morning afternoon hours on May 4th.  These storms produced gusts of 40 to 50 MPH but a few of these storms became severe and produced hail to 1 inch in diameter and damaging straight-line winds.","213574":"A few severe thunderstorms producing wind damage developed over Macomb, St. Clair, and Wayne counties.","213576":"Thunderstorms moving through Lake St. Clair produced wind gusts in excess of 45 knots.","213844":"An advancing warm front triggered widely scattered thunderstorms across parts of central Illinois during the evening of May 3rd into the pre-dawn hours of May 4th.  One cell produced penny-sized hail in Coles County.","213964":"The 2026 spring breakup along the Yukon River was highly dynamic, defined by a combination of thermal ice decay and severe mechanical ice jams. Because of an exceptionally cold winter and a cool spring, the river ice was thicker than normal and had not deteriorated significantly before the main breakup front arrived. Early-season flooding was driven by localized snowmelt and ice jams on independent river systems and upper sections of the Yukon River.","214671":"Heavy rains from late April into May led to river flooding on the Cache River at the Patterson gage."},"events":[{"begin":"2026-05-04T00:05Z","county":"Josephine","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"10.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-05-04T00:30Z","endLat":42.3745,"endLon":-123.457,"episode":211892,"id":1324272,"injuries":0,"lat":42.3745,"location":"0.76 ESE Wilderville","lon":-123.457,"magnitude":60.0,"magnitudeType":"EG","narrative":"A thunderstorm produced strong winds in Grants Pass. Damage included a tree falling on and damaging a home.","source":"NWS Storm Survey","state":"OR","type"